 ## Overview
-Deferred Voxel Shading is a real-time global illumination technique that merges voxel cone tracing with deferred shading to compute indirect lighting effects such as diffuse/specular reflection, color blending, emissive materials, and shadows [S2, unofficial]. It solves the problem of redundant lighting calculations in scenes with many light sources by delaying lighting computations until after all geometry is rendered, allowing voxel cone tracing to be performed as a screenspace post-process [S1, unofficial]. Unlike forward voxel cone tracing, it integrates direct lighting into the voxel structure during voxelization, enabling emissive surfaces to contribute to indirect illumination even when occluded [S2, unofficial].
+Deferred Voxel Shading is a real-time global illumination technique that merges voxel cone tracing with deferred shading to compute indirect lighting effects such as diffuse/specular reflection, color blending, emissive materials, and shadows [S2, unofficial]. It solves the problem of redundant lighting calculations in scenes with many light sources by delaying lighting computations until after all geometry is rendered, allowing voxel cone tracing to be performed as a screenspace post-process [S1, unofficial]. Unlike forward voxel cone tracing, it integrates direct lighting into the voxel structure during voxelization, enabling emissive surfaces to contribute to indirect illumination even when occluded [S2, unofficial]. It builds on [Voxel Cone Tracing](1454-voxel-cone-tracing.md) and differs from siblings like [Customized Cone Tracing](3447-customized-cone-tracing.md) and [Voxel Shading via Convolution](4778-voxel-shading-via-convolution.md) by computing direct illumination per voxel and using anisotropic directional volumes for cone sampling.
 
